WEBVTT
TITLE Fixed Point Arithmetic — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
A fixed-point number is an ordinary integer with the binary point nailed to one place.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Eight bits with four after the point: divide the stored integer by sixteen to get the value.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.000
The 256 patterns become 256 values, spaced exactly one sixteenth apart, from minus 8 to 7.9375.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.000
The hardware did not change. The scale is a promise the programmer keeps.

5
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Read the bits with weights: 8, 4, 2, 1, then a half, a quarter, an eighth and a sixteenth.

6
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:40.000
0110.1000 sums to 6.5: four, plus two, plus one half.

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:47.000
The leftmost bit weighs minus eight, so 1000.0000 is exactly minus 8.

8
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.000
All ones is minus one sixteenth: the value closest to zero from below.

9
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.000
The negative half holds 128 patterns and the non-negative half 127, so plus 8 does not exist.

10
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Width is fixed, so every fraction bit you add is an integer bit you give up.

11
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Q2.6: six fraction bits give a step of 1/64, but the window is only minus 2 to 1.98.

12
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Q6.2: a step of 1/4, and a window of minus 32 to 31.75. Same eight bits, sixteen times coarser.

13
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Resolution and range trade one for one in bits: i plus f equals n, always.

14
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
So the choice of f is a design decision, not a detail.

15
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Addition needs both points in the same column, so both operands must carry the same f.

16
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.000
6.5 plus 5.25: align the points, then add the stored integers, 104 plus 84 is 188.

17
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.000
188 is 11.75 with the point still four bits from the right: the scale never changes under addition.

18
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.000
But 11.75 is outside minus 8 to 7.9375, so eight bits cannot hold it: the sum needs one more integer bit.

19
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Adding k values needs i plus log base two of k integer bits: computed here for 2, 4, 8 and 16.

20
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Kept in eight bits, 188 is the pattern 1011.1100, which decodes to minus 4.25.

21
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
The value did not get smaller; the top bit was read as a sign. That is wraparound, not saturation.

22
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.000
One quarter of all 65,536 pairs overflow: 16,384 of them, and each of those wraps.

23
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.000
The exact check compares the sum against the window before storing, never the sign of the result alone.

24
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Saturation clamps to 7.9375 instead of wrapping. It is a choice, and both are in use.

25
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Multiplication is where the binary point moves: the fraction bits add.

26
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Q4.4 times Q4.4 gives Q8.8: a sixteen-bit product whose point sits eight bits from the right.

27
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.000
1.1875 is 19 sixteenths; 19 times 19 is 361, and 361 over 256 is 1.41015625 exactly.

28
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.000
To store it back in Q4.4 you shift right by four, which drops four bits you cannot keep.

29
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Truncating gives 22 sixteenths, 1.375. Rounding first gives 23 sixteenths, 1.4375. Both miss by under one step.

30
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Dropping bits with a right shift is a floor: it always moves toward minus infinity.

31
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Over all 65,536 products, truncation loses 13/512 of a step on average: a bias that always points down.

32
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Adding half a step before the shift changes the mean error to plus 1/256, and the worst to 1/32.

33
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Truncation was exact for 12,288 products, but exactness is not the point: the bias is.

34
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.000
In a loop that feeds its own output back, that bias accumulates into a visible offset.

35
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.000
There is a second trap: an arithmetic right shift floors, while integer division truncates toward zero.

36
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Minus 9 sixteenths is minus 0.5625. Halving it by shift gives minus 5 sixteenths, minus 0.3125.

37
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Truncating division gives minus 4 sixteenths, minus 0.25. The two answers differ by exactly one step.

38
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.000
That happens for 64 of the 256 patterns, so a sign-blind rescale is wrong on a quarter of the negatives.

39
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.000
Choose one rule, state it, and check it on a negative value before trusting the loop.

40
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.000
0.1 has no exact eight-bit binary fraction: it stores as 26/256, which is 0.1015625.

41
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Adding it ten times gives 260/256, that is 1.015625 instead of 1: a 1.5625 per cent drift.

42
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.000
The drift is the encoding error times the number of additions, so it grows with the loop.

43
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Sizing the accumulator from the count is the fix: k additions need i plus log base two of k integer bits.

44
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.000
Q1.15 shows the same economics at sixteen bits: range minus 1 to 0.99997, step 2 to the minus 15.

45
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Fixed point spends its bits on one grid: the step is the same everywhere.

46
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Floating point spends them on a ratio: near zero the steps are tiny, far out they are huge.

47
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.000
So fixed point has constant absolute error and floating point has constant relative error.

48
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.000
At the smallest positive value, half a step is 50 per cent of it; at 4 it is under 1 per cent.

49
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.000
That single sentence decides which of the two representations a design should use.

50
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Every word-width question on this topic follows the same five steps.

51
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.000
Name n, f and i; write the window and the step; then apply the operation’s own rule.

52
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Addition keeps f and costs an integer bit; multiplication adds f and doubles n.

53
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.000
Round before you shift, and saturate at the boundary rather than wrap.

54
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Five steps, every time, with the numbers written down.

55
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.000
Hold five sentences: the point is fixed, the step is 2^-f, and i plus f is n.

56
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:32.000
Add with equal f; multiply and the f’s add; a right shift means floor.

57
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Round before discarding, saturate at the edge, and size the accumulator from the count.

